              {
                "text": "I'm testing whether right-side objects near a dividing line get mirrored to the left. Checking coordinates: the 4 moved from the right side to the left side in the output, while the 9 and 7 stayed put, so I'm trying to figure out what distinguishes the moved objects from the stationary ones, and now checking whether the 2 also moved.\n\nNeither straight mirroring nor consistent offsets explain the placements, so I'm considering whether the rule is that objects with no matching row-pair on the opposite side get relocated left, while ones with an existing counterpart stay fixed. That fits rows 2-3 (empty left side, 4 moves) and rows 6-7 (8 already there, 9 stays), but rows 10-11 has both 5 already present and two right-side objects, one of which moved, so I need another explanation for why 2 specifically shifted while 7 didn't.\n\nThen I notice both moved elements shifted left by exactly 10 columns -- 4 from cols 15-16 to 5-6, and 2 from 16-17 to 6-7 -- suggesting the right panel (cols 10-18) is being translated onto the left panel (cols 0-8) as if the grid splits at col 9. Testing this against 9 at 16-17 mapping to 6-7 reveals a conflict since 8 already occupies that spot there.",
